<h2>How the dispute arose</h2>
<p>Geetagram Cooperative Housing Society sued the heirs of Purshottam Punjabhai in Civil Suit No. 322 of 2012 for specific performance of a 1996 agreement to sell. The suit ended quickly: a consent decree followed on 29 April 2013. Execution, however, waited years, and a sale deed was eventually executed in June 2022 in favour of the appellant through a Court Commissioner, with the Society as confirming party.</p>
<p>Members of a Hindu Undivided Family who were never parties to that suit asserted that the decreed property was theirs, tracing their claim to a 1975 sale and mutation entries. In 2024, the High Court condoned their delay of roughly eleven years, granted them leave to appeal against the consent decree, and restrained the appellant from dealing with the land. The appellant carried both orders to the Supreme Court, arguing that a consent decree is unappealable by force of Section 96(3) CPC.</p>

<h2>The statutory knot</h2>
<p>Section 96(3) CPC declares that no appeal shall lie from a decree passed with the consent of parties. Order XXIII Rule 3A separately bars a suit to set aside a compromise decree on the ground that the compromise was not lawful. Read together, these provisions channel a party aggrieved by a compromise back to the court that recorded it. The question was whether they also close the door on a person who never joined the compromise.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The Court answered that they do not. A stranger to a compromise decree cannot be said to be barred under Section 96(3), because the foundation of the bar is consent — and a non-party, the Court observed, cannot, merely by virtue of the decree having been passed with the consent of others, be deemed to have consented to it.</p>
</div>

<h2>The framework the Court laid down</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Parties to the compromise.</strong> Bound by Section 96(3). Their remedy lies before the court that recorded the compromise, on the footing that no valid compromise existed — not in an appeal.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Persons claiming through a party.</strong> Stand in the shoes of their predecessor. Authority holding that such persons cannot appeal a consent decree was distinguished on exactly this basis.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>True strangers.</strong> May seek leave to appeal. They must show, prima facie, that the decree is one by which they are aggrieved, prejudicially affected, or bound as res judicata under Explanation 6 to Section 11 CPC.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The filter of leave.</strong> Leave is never automatic. Following the settled line of authority on appeals by non-parties, leave is refused to those only remotely or indirectly affected; a legally cognisable interest must be demonstrated.</div>
</div>
<p>Applying that framework, the Court found the HUF members had asserted exactly such an interest — a recorded sale, mutation entries, and a sequence of later dealings that, if their version holds, the consent decree would override. Whether their title claim succeeds is for the High Court; their standing to be heard in appeal could not be denied. The eleven-year delay was also left condoned, the explanation of when they learned of the decree having been accepted.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<p>Consent decrees are a familiar instrument in property practice, and an equally familiar vehicle for grievance: persons out of the record sometimes allege that a collusive suit and a swift compromise were used to create title over land they claim. This judgment confirms the procedural answer. The affected outsider does not have to mount a fresh suit that Order XXIII Rule 3A may complicate; an appeal with leave under Section 96 is maintainable, and the compromise character of the decree is no shield against appellate scrutiny at the threshold.</p>
<p>At the same time, the decision is no charter for officious interlopers. The requirement of leave does real work: the applicant must put a concrete, legally protected interest on the table and show prima facie prejudice flowing from the decree itself. Those who fail that test remain outside the appeal.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>For a non-party who discovers a compromise decree touching his property: move promptly for leave to appeal, plead the precise interest affected, and explain the date of knowledge candidly — limitation runs from facts the court will test.</p>
<p>For decree-holders: when a compromise involves property with a contested history, joining all persons with visible claims of record spares the decree years of vulnerability to leave applications and injunctions.</p>
<p>For parties to a compromise: the route remains the recording court itself; Section 96(3) continues to bar their appeal.</p>
</div>
